  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain is expected Sunday night into Monday \nafternoon. Enough rain to make a small dent in the drought is \nlikely with some places getting over one inch.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nModels have trended further south with the low pressure this \ncycle. Still some uncertainty on where exactly the heaviest \nrain will fall, but have confidence in Downeast region seeing \nthe heavier rain totals. \n\nThe general consensus is for the low pressure center to track \nnortheast just south of the Downeast coast late Sunday night \ninto Monday morning. The low could still track north or south \nof this best guess track, but models have been trending south. \nJust north of the track of the center of the low pressure, we \nexpect a band of heavy rain to set up, with totals up to about \n1.5 inches, all falling within about 9 hours. A few \nthunderstorms are even possible along and immediately ahead of \nthe low pressure over the outer waters. \n\nFor rain totals, we are looking at least 0.5 inch for all areas\nexcept the North Woods where totals could be much less. Over 1 \ninch is expected (80 percent chance) south of Lincoln. Totals up\nto around 2 inches are possible, with the best chance of a 2 \ninch total south of Bangor, where the probability is 30-60 \npercent chance, depending on where the heaviest banded \nprecipitation sets up just north of the low pressure track. With\nit having been so dry, we do not anticipate impacts other than \nminor flooding of areas with poor drainage.\n\nThere is no risk of snow with this system, as the airmass will\nbe quite mild for this time of year. \n\nWinds along/north of the low pressure track appear pretty light\nthrough Monday morning, but anywhere south of the low track \ncould have some decent south winds and should be limited to the\nouter waters.\n\nModels have trended a bit faster with the end time to the rain,\nwith rain tapering off from west to east probably between 8am \nand noon Monday. Colder air starts moving in late Monday \nafternoon, and by dawn Tuesday temperatures will be a bit below \nfreezing for most areas. Quite cold from Tuesday into Wednesday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An active weather pattern is likely from late on\nThanksgiving into next weekend. Forecast confidence on any \nparticular weather system is low, but travelers should continue \nto monitor this period for potential wintry weather.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nThe general weather pattern from Thanksgiving through the \nweekend after Thanksgiving looks favorable for active weather. \nThis is important given the active travel period. Various \nensemble guidance does continue to be favorable for systems to \ndevelop but high uncertainty still with the timing and strength \nof the systems.\n\nThe first potential Noreaster is late Thanksgiving through\nFriday night, perhaps lingering into Saturday. Models have \ntrended further north with the track of this system, and now \nmost models have decent precipitation during this period. The \nissue is now mostly whether or not it may be too warm for snow. \nIf the system is on the colder side, substantial snow is \npossible, but it could just as easily be mostly rain for most of\nthe area. Odds favor rain Downeast and tilt more toward snow in\nthe far north, but it is hard to get more specific than this. \nIt is simply too early to know with such significant uncertainty\nin the track, strength, and timing of the system.\n\nIn summary, it is still too early to be overly alarmed or alter\ntravel plans, but be aware of the potential for active weather\nthat could impact travel Thanksgiving evening through Sunday, \nand continue to check back with us for updates.",
